Fillets: a walked blend is split at a point only where its contacts meet
there (mitcad#1420).

ChFi3d_FilBuilder::SplitSurf splits a walked blend whose contacts came
close (BlendFunc's minimal distance within 100 times the approximation's
tolerance) at the points where its iso is a point: it takes each minimum of
the distance between the contacts along the walk, finds it exactly with
ChFi3d_SearchSing and keeps it where that function's value was below the
tolerance. That value is the derivative of half the squared distance,
nought at every minimum: any minimum split the blend, however far apart
the contacts were there, its two pieces on two surfaces (two faces) about a
vertex whose tolerance was that distance.

A ball that stays put (1520) keeps its contacts a constant distance apart,
and the walk's noise makes minima of it. A lid's top rim rounded with 1
through the short arc between the top and an earlier vertical rounding of
1 and the mitre of that rounding with the one along the top (the contacts
meeting where the mitre ends at the flat side): the walk was split at the
arc's end, the contacts 1.414 apart there, and each corner's piece of the
ball's sphere was two faces (0.026460 and 1.590495 mm2) where the stored
body has one (1.616266 mm2).

The split is now taken only where the contacts lie within the distance the
walk took for singular (100 times the approximation's tolerance) of each
other: each corner is one face (1.615949 mm2).

Test: geometry/tests/test_fillet_stationary_ball.cpp (each corner one face,
the case's result with the stored body's faces).

Upstream: new.

